  "headline": "A strong dollar and $110 oil are the squeeze, but markets are not pricing fear",
  "body": [
    "The dashboard describes a dollar-and-energy squeeze, not a growth scare. Brent at $110, up 82% year to date, and a Dollar Index sitting at the very top of its 52-week range are the tightening forces here, and they bite hardest abroad. Yet gold is pinned at 8% of its own range while copper sits near the top of its range, which says risk appetite is intact and inflation pressure is arriving through energy rather than broad demand.",
    "Gold is the tell. It has fallen 5.8% over the month while the 10-year yield dropped 19 basis points, a pairing that only makes sense when a haven bid is absent and the dollar is doing the damage. Bitcoin buys 2% more gold than a month ago and the copper-to-gold ratio is up 4% over the same period: liquidity and growth are winning over fear.",
    "The tension is between crude and bonds. Oil rose 13.5% over the month while the 30-year yield fell 20 basis points, and the 30-10 curve barely moved at 31 basis points. Bonds are reading expensive crude as a tax on demand rather than inflation to chase, and with the 10-year already up 83 basis points this year, that reading carries more weight than the oil chart alone: a cooling, not a collapse.",
    "That is where the news matters. Drop Site News reports that the UAE is covertly providing targeting help to Ansarallah against Saudi-backed forces in Yemen, and separately characterizes the Saudi war there as a quagmire. The channel is Saudi Arabia's southern flank and Red Sea shipping, and Brent's realized volatility is calm at roughly half its own year's median, which suggests the market is carrying a supply premium but has not priced a widening Gulf conflict. That is bullish for Brent, supportive for the Canadian dollar (USD/CAD is already down 0.9% over the month against a rising dollar), and a headwind for the rupee as an oil importer, with USD/INR up 6% year to date.",
    "Taken together, the US is the relative winner: dollar at its highs, copper up 18% year to date, long yields near 5% without the curve blowing out. The cost is being exported through the yen and the rupee rather than felt at home. The risk to this read runs through the Yemen channel: if that conflict widens, oil shifts from a tax on demand to a shock, and the bond market would have to abandon its benign reading, taking gold's haven bid with it."
